plan LazyFeatures
title   Lazy-loaded features
status  proposed
goal
    An app can defer feature code without losing edits, changing synchronous actor APIs, or
    allowing undeclared machines to run. Opening a feature restores its saved state without
    replaying work. Lazy features remain inspectable before an end user opens them.
outcomes
    O1  Lazy entries
        Alongside eager entries, app({ id, machines }) accepts { id, load, context? }.
        id is the machine's definition id; load is () => Promise<Machine> from a build-local
        import(). Remote entries are outside version 1. context is the entry's wiring default.
        Eager entry forms are @plan.Runtime.O1.

        The loader's result must have the declared literal id. A wrong id, non-machine result,
        wrong context provider or missing runtimeSetup service is a type error. Service
        requirements include lazy machines even before they are adopted. Entries have no
        services field or per-entry Layer. A service that loads heavy code on first call reports
        a load failure as its own failure: a lookup's Failure lane, or a commit's typed failure,
        never unknown, because nothing was sent.

        An undeclared machine cannot run. Declaring the same machine twice rejects construction.
        A ref already carries its machine's code: normal actor calls and hooks never call load.
        There is no runtime.load, loading actor lifecycle or separate lazy hook.
    O2  Adoption
        When ensureActor(ref) or createActor(machine) first encounters a declared lazy machine,
        it adopts that entry synchronously before admitting the actor. Wiring that reaches lazy
        providers adopts those providers too. Eager wiring or setup that already holds a lazy
        machine adopts it during readiness. Readiness requirements are @plan.Runtime.O2.

        Adoption accepts only the declared id and a Flow machine; a loader result with another id 
        fails
        with EntryMismatch. The first accepted machine
        object binds that id in this Runtime; any later different object, including a loader's
        result, fails with EntryMismatch. Repeated adoption of the same object is harmless.
        Disposing all its actors does not deactivate the entry.

        Adoption enforces the same cross-machine validity rules as eager construction, including
        context cycles and conflicting machine, operation, domain, entity or resource identities.
        A failure exposes none of the new actors, restored data or provider closure; the entry
        remains inactive and a later trigger can retry. A host call throws the FlowError; hook
        error delivery is @plan.React.O5. Successful adoption exposes the entire closure and its
        restored state together; no actor observes a partly adopted feature.

        Render, lookup-only hooks, getSnapshot, useView, runtime inspection and capture never
        adopt. An actor's send(ref, ...) never adopts or creates its target: an absent target
        drops the send and reports SendTargetAbsent, with details.adopted false for an inactive
        entry. Hook first-render behavior is @plan.React.O2; pre-attach sends are @plan.React.O3.

        Adopting the same entries in a different order does not change app behavior except through
        restored data or availability of an entity's lazy .byKey loader. Before that loader is
        adopted, repairs use active answers or drop unconfirmed fields rather than show stale
        truth indefinitely.
    O3  Dormant records
        Persisted records belonging to unopened features remain dormant: readiness does not load
        their code, and captures preserve their saved contents until adoption. Unclaimed operation
        and entity records remain dormant until their owner is adopted or a version change
        discards the scope. Actor records whose machine id no entry declares are orphans,
        found at ready without loading code; @plan.Persistence.O5 discards them.

        Adoption restores dormant data under @plan.Persistence.O4. A restored consumer takes its
        context from current providers without emitting a spurious context change. A dormant
        record that fails to decode at adoption is discarded alone with a diagnostic and its actor
        opens fresh; other records and live committed truth are untouched (@plan.Persistence.O5).
        Persisted data never makes a feature unopenable.

        A saved uncertain write marks affected entities uncertain no later than when those records
        first become readable, even while its actor remains dormant. Repair starts as soon as
        those entities are known, using available loaders and active answers; the actor receives
        its unknown outcome when adopted. Entity repair behavior is @plan.EntityDomains.O1.
        A Runtime refused persistence has no dormant records; second-tab behavior is
        @plan.Persistence.O7. Dormant data and adopted actors never cross Runtime, request or
        principal boundaries.
    O4  Lazy features and server rendering
        Server preparation with refs adopts synchronously. Transferred lazy entry ids must name
        entries declared lazy in the client app; otherwise hydration rejects with
        HydrationMalformed. Payloads cannot choose arbitrary code to import.

        Before adoption, a hook holding the ref can read the transferred server-view snapshot
        without registering the feature. Hydration's no-mismatch behavior is
        @plan.ServerRendering.O4; merge precedence is @plan.ServerRendering.O5.

        Client ready() calls load for transferred inactive entries in parallel and adopts
        successful results in app declaration order before resolving. A Hydrate boundary committed 
        after ready loads its inactive transferred entries the same
        way in its commit effect. Each load has a 10-second bound. A rejected or
        timed-out load reports EntryLoadFailed with its cause, but does not fail ready(). Its entry
        stays inactive, transferred state remains provisional and persisted data stays dormant.
        The first later attach holding its ref adopts it and installs that state under the same
        merge rules; a failed load never loses the transferred truth. No automatic retry runs.

        Stories never call load, including during hydration. A transferred lazy entry remains
        inactive until a Story command holding its machine or ref adopts it.
    O5  Tools and checks
        Structural inspection is @plan.Inspection.O1: inspect(app) returns Promise<Model> and
        loads every declared entry, including unopened features. A rejected load rejects inspection
        with EntryLoadFailed naming the entry. Its model matches the equivalent eager app except
        for lazy-loading information. Live inspection and Story checkpoints expose which entries
        are adopted without adopting them.

        flow check validates the fully loaded app under @plan.CommandLine.O5, so unopened code
        cannot hide invalid wiring or identity collisions. A mismatched id reports EntryMismatch;
        a failed load reports EntryLoadFailed and makes the check fail. It warns when an entity
        depends on a lazy .byKey loader, eager wiring defeats the code split, or load is not a
        literal import() optionally selecting an export.
verification
    V1  Type tests reject wrong ids, results, context and missing services; inference works.
    V2  A bundle test defers feature code while preserving the eager app's behavior.
    V3  Runtime tests prove synchronous adoption, provider closure, rollback and idempotence;
        adoption order never changes the compiled graph, and a context cycle reachable only
        through a newly adopted closure is rejected.
    V4  Stories prove reads and sends never adopt; adoption order obeys O2's exceptions.
    V5  Restore tests prove dormant edits survive, corruption opens fresh and uncertainty repairs.
    V6  Hydration tests prove bounded loads, matching render and later attach without data loss.
    V7  Tool tests prove unopened features remain inspectable and checked, including load failures.
questions
    Q1  Is 10 seconds the right bound for a post-SSR load?
        The current bound prevents a stalled import from holding ready() open indefinitely;
        validate it against real client loading times before release.
    Q2  Deferred to build time
        Compilation caching and graph representation.
        Admission staging, rollback and publication mechanics.
        Dormant storage layout and capture encoding.
        Uncertainty-marker encoding.
        Loader concurrency and clock integration.
        Heavy service implementation imports and failure caching.
        Adoption trace fields and diagram styling.
        Static-analysis recognition of loader syntax.
        Bundle proving hosts and test-generation strategy.
